From: yzddmr6 <46088090+yzddmr6@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] gpu: present virgl scanouts zero-copy through
 linux-dmabuf

Linux guests' desktops run on virgl, so every scanout is a classic
resource: a host GL texture rendered by virglrenderer. 0040 imported only
blob scanouts, and these textures took the copy path: a glReadPixels of
the whole frame on the GPU worker, a copy into the compositor's memory
texture and an upload, for every presented frame (about 2 GB/s for a
window-sized 2560x1600 guest at 120 Hz).

iceland gvm 0002 reported that importing these textures gave black or
frozen windows, and 0008 that idle compositors stayed black. GunyahVM
1.4.0's experimental import (CROSVM_GVM_SCANOUT_IMPORT=1) did not show
black windows in six starts on the OnePlus Pad 2 Pro.

The import needs the texture's real layout. rutabaga exports these
textures without the stride and records width * 4, but a linear Adreno
texture's pitch is aligned to 64 pixels, so any width that is not a
multiple of 64 (a window-sized guest) would be sheared. gvm_export asks
virglrenderer for the dmabuf with the stride and offset the host EGL
reports; the modifier is the one rutabaga queried at creation. A GLES host
stores the guest's B8G8R8X8 scanout as an RGBA8 texture, which the host
EGL exports as ABGR8888, and the compositor would blend with whatever the
fourth byte held. A texture whose guest format has no alpha is presented
with the matching X fourcc.
Textures stored bottom row first, resources the compositor refuses and
resources larger than the scanout keep the copy path. The display
reconnection released imports with the surface and import ids swapped.

CROSVM_GVM_SCANOUT_IMPORT=0 copies every frame, for comparison only.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
